-- title:
--   1-wU and 1-wV are non-zero-divisors in the crossing quotient
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring, let $t \in W$ be a non-zero-divisor, and let $w \in W$ be arbitrary. Write $\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\ W\ t$ for the quotient of the polynomial ring $W[X_0,X_1]$ in two variables (indexed by `Fin 2`) by the principal ideal generated by $X_0X_1 - t$, a $W$-algebra via the structure map `algebraMap W (CrossingQuotient W t)`, and let `U t` and `V t` denote the two distinguished elements of this quotient attached to $t$. The assertion is the conjunction of two statements: the element $1 - w \cdot U t$ lies in the submonoid `nonZeroDivisors (CrossingQuotient W t)` of non-zero-divisors of the quotient, and likewise $1 - w \cdot V t$ lies in `nonZeroDivisors (CrossingQuotient W t)`; here $w$ acts through its image under the structure map. Thus multiplication by either of these two elements is injective on $W[X_0,X_1]/(X_0X_1 - t)$.
--
--   This is an elementary non-zero-divisor statement for the rings $W[x,y]/(xy-t)$ that model a node, as they occur in semistable models of curves. It is used in the construction of resolutions of the crossing quotient, where it feeds the invertibility statement [`MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.Resolution.isInvertible_ker_section`](thm.html#MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.Resolution.isInvertible_ker_section); the monomial basis of the quotient supplied by [`MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.linearIndependent_monomial_and_span_eq_top`](thm.html#MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.linearIndependent_monomial_and_span_eq_top) is what makes it accessible.
